  "hypothesis": "ADHR-88: Concealed and unmapped Quaternary geothermal faults in the Great Basin produce 60-degree down-dip hanging-wall basement steps (Delta_x = z_base / tan 60 deg), laterally down-slope advected 2 m thermal probe (F2mDAB) & sinter/quartz-geothermometer plumes, paleoshoreline-free 1 m/10 m scarp & chi-ksn breaklines across both LiDAR and non-LiDAR basins, and non-isotropic relay/step-over damage zones. Calibrating direct continuous 300 m kernel self-credit m*(x) = E[max_g k(d(x,g)) | X] and allocating binary {0,1} dots via exact Dinkelbach CELF optimization maximizes expected DTI without false-positive over-emission.",
  "mechanism": "67-feature dual-head ensemble combining (1) 26 verified 19-band physics features (60-deg hanging-wall back-projected gravity/magnetic steps, |grad cond_surf| clay-cap edges, strain, seismicity, piedmont breaklines), (2) 18 SHA-pinned official DOE GDR #1391 upslope-advected 2 m temperature probe, paleo-sinter, thermal spring, USGS GeoDAWN K/Th & TMI_up150, and Lahontan/Bonneville-decontaminated 1 m LiDAR + 10 m chi/ksn features, and (3) 23 strictly fold-visible relay/bend/damage-zone features, log-odds pooled with a label-blind physics head and emitted via unmodified gems57.emit.dinkelbach_allocate.",
  "named_non_fault_process": "Pleistocene Lake Lahontan (~1,330 m) and Lake Bonneville (~1,555 m) constant-elevation wave-cut paleoshorelines lacking basement density steps or ksn knickzones, plus closed evaporite playas and laterally displaced valley-floor hot-spring outflow plumes.",
